{"ts": 1789771087.462966, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The picture is consistent and stable. Let me settle on the specification and reason carefully about the OOS forecast.\n\n**Chosen spec: features [5, 37, 25] with signs [+1, \u22121, \u22121], in-sample Sharpe 1.202.**\n\nWhy I'm fairly confident this is real signal rather than a fluke:\n- Its three components were the top three singles *independently*, before any combination search. I didn't discover it by dredging pairs.\n- Sign flips destroy it (1.202 \u2192 0.37), so the directions are well-identified.\n- Substituting any of ~20 other features into the third slot yields ~0.93\u20131.01, clustered tightly below the pair baseline. The winner isn't perched on a lone spike in the search space.\n\n**Now the honest part \u2014 how much to discount.** I should *not* predict 1.20. Several forces push OOS down:\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I evaluated ~60 specs and am reporting the max. Even with a real underlying edge, the maximum of a noisy set overstates the truth. With n=5000 daily obs (~20y), SE on a Sharpe \u2248 \u221a(1/20) \u2248 0.22. Picking the best of many draws with that noise inflates the estimate by roughly 0.2\u20130.3.\n\n2. **Correlated-feature bias.** My 40 singles and the combination search aren't independent tests, which softens the multiple-comparisons penalty somewhat, but doesn't eliminate it.\n\n3. **Regime/structural decay.** OOS data is genuinely unseen. Real-world feature relationships degrade; equal weights fit in-sample tend not to travel perfectly.\n\nNetting these, I'll shade the in-sample 1.202 down substantially. I estimate the true underlying Sharpe near ~0.85, and set my point forecast slightly below that at **0.85**, reflecting that the selected max overstates truth by roughly 0.3.\n\nFor uncertainty: pure sampling noise alone gives ~0.22. Adding uncertainty about how much the edge decays OOS and possible regime shift, I widen to **0.35**. That puts a ~68% interval of roughly [0.50, 1.20] and leaves meaningful probability on a mediocre ~0.3 outcome \u2014 which I think is appropriately humble given I only observed one in-sample period."}
